Make regscscf.dereg_impu's NOTIFY carry the contact it de-registered.

reg_rpc_dereg_impu() marks a contact CONTACT_DELETE_PENDING, queues a
reg-event notification for it, and sets CONTACT_DELETED on the next
statement. Only the queueing happens here: the body is rendered later, in
the forked notification worker, by generate_reginfo_full(), and
process_xml_for_contact() returns early for CONTACT_DELETED. So the NOTIFY
that goes out names the registration

    <registration aor="sip:..." state="terminated"></registration>

with no <contact> child at all.

This is not a race the worker can win. The two statements are separated by
nothing, and both run while the RPC process holds lock_contact_slot_i() on
that contact and lock_udomain() on the IMPU -- the same two locks
generate_reginfo_full() takes before it reads the state. The worker is
excluded until after CONTACT_DELETED is committed, every time.

A watcher that only removes a binding it can find in the body -- which is
what ims_registrar_pcscf does, notify.c process_contact() -- therefore keeps
its contact, and on an IMS access leg keeps the IPsec SA bound to it. The UE
stays reachable on paper at a P-CSCF whose S-CSCF has forgotten it, until
the contact expires on its own.

Pass the contact URI as an explicit de-registration contact instead of
relying on the usrloc walk. new_notification() copies the URI into the
queued notification, and generate_reginfo_full() emits it through
process_xml_for_explit_dereg_contact(), which renders state="terminated"
event="expired" expires="0" from that copy and never looks at usrloc. The
body is then independent of when the render happens, which is the same
reason the de-REGISTER path in save.c produces a usable body:
update_contacts_sar_user_deregistration() collects the Contact headers into
explicit_dereg_contact before it starts flipping states, and its own live
walk contributes nothing by the time the worker runs either.

Nothing else changes. The contact still goes to CONTACT_DELETED here, so
timer_impurecord() unlinks it on the next tick exactly as before; only the
notification body is fixed.

